  collector is down, and a privacy review — or none of that.

  - [1mConsole only, with levels and namespaces.[0m Buys a clean logger and quieter default output; costs nothing; rules out
    ever seeing a production failure you didn't reproduce yourself. Honest framing: this is a [3mcode hygiene[0m change, not a
    production-debugging one. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mShip to your own collector.[0m Batched [38;5;153mPOST[39m ([38;5;153mnavigator.sendBeacon[39m on unload) to an endpoint you run. Buys real
    production visibility; costs a backend endpoint that must exist, plus retry/drop policy and a decision about PII.
    Note: no such endpoint exists in this repo — you'd be committing to standing one up.
  - [1mThird-party SaaS (Sentry / LogRocket / Datadog RUM).[0m Buys production visibility plus stack traces, session context,
    and alerting on day one; costs an external dependency and sending user data to a vendor. This repo has zero
    dependencies and no build step, so it'd be a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag from a CDN.
  - [1mLocal buffer + export.[0m Keep the last N entries in memory/[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, expose a "download diagnostics" action.
    Buys logs from users who report a bug; costs nothing external; misses everyone who doesn't report.

  I recommend the [1mthird-party SaaS[0m route if production visibility is the real goal — it's the only option that gets you
  stack traces and alerting without you building and operating a log pipeline. If you already run a collector, its
  option becomes the cheaper equivalent. I'd steer away from console-only unless you're really asking for cleanup rather
  than observability.
